# Containerfile Layer Template Schema
#
# `Containerfile.layer` is not an image of its own — it is the template for an
# image that adds one harness CLI to the dev base image. The base image is the
# first part of a set; this file is how every later part attaches to it, so the
# rules it encodes are a contract between two artifacts rather than a build
# recipe.
#
# Build it two ways, with one argument either way:
#
#   docker build -f Containerfile.layer \
#     --build-arg AGENT_DEV_BASE_REF=<registry>/<namespace>/<name>@sha256:<manifest list digest> \
#     -t <your-tag> .                       # published base — pin the digest
#
#   docker build -f Containerfile.layer \
#     --build-arg AGENT_DEV_BASE_REF=<local name>:<version>-<commit> \
#     -t <your-tag> .                       # locally built base — the tag is the reference

## The Base Reference

One build argument, `AGENT_DEV_BASE_REF`, carrying a complete image reference.
Both forms below are complete; a single `FROM` cannot take an optional `@`, so
the reference is passed whole rather than assembled from two arguments:

| Form | Value | When |
|------|-------|------|
| Digest | `<registry>/<namespace>/<name>@sha256:<manifest list digest>` | Whenever the base came from a registry. This is the *only* form that pins: a digest cannot move, and the manifest list digest resolves correctly on every platform. |
| Tag | `<name>:<version>-<commit>` | When the base was built on this machine and never pushed. No registry, no namespace, no login, no credential — and no pin, which the layer's own notes must say rather than imply. |

Pin the manifest list digest, never a tag and never a per-platform digest. A
tag moves; a per-platform digest pins one architecture and makes the other
architecture's build fail with `no matching manifest`. The manifest list digest
is the only pin that resolves correctly on both platforms. When a base is
unpublished there is no digest to pin — say the pin is unverified instead of
claiming it, and re-pin to the digest form the moment the base is published.

A layer needs no BuildKit: the instructions a layer adds (`RUN`, `USER`,
`ENV`, `COPY`, `HEALTHCHECK`) are all understood by the classic builder, and the
template carries no `# syntax=` directive that would demand otherwise. Add one
deliberately if your install step wants `RUN --mount=type=cache`.

## What a Layer May Change

- the packages it installs, in its own `RUN` steps;
- the harness CLI: its installation, its version, its configuration defaults;
- `AGENT_HARNESS`, which is how the inherited entrypoint finds the CLI;
- optionally `CMD`, and optionally a `HEALTHCHECK`;
- optionally a wrapper script, when the harness needs default arguments (the
  base forwards only the container's own arguments — see
  `entrypoint.sh.schema`).

## What a Layer Must Not Change

| Do not | Why |
|--------|-----|
| Re-declare or rename the runtime account | The account, its ids, and its home are the base's contract (R-13); a consumer pinning two layers of the same set would get two different identities. |
| Replace or wrap the entrypoint | The run contract — env names, refusal codes, `exec` semantics — is what makes the parts composable; a wrapper that swallows signals or masks exit status breaks supervision. |
| Change the workspace default path | Consumers mount the workspace at the base's default unless they override it deliberately. |
| Install the harness as the runtime account into a root-owned directory | The install step belongs to a `USER root` section that ends with a switch back to `${AGENT_USER}`; a half-switched layer installs fine and fails at run time. |
| Bake credentials, tokens, or a `.env` into the layer | The set is secret-free by construction; credentials arrive at run time (R-9). |
| Leave the harness unpinned | A layer whose CLI version floats makes a rebuild a different image; the layer's own tag scheme (version plus commit) is what makes it auditable. |

## Why This Shape

- **One complete reference, `FROM` it as it comes.** Two arguments (a name plus
  a digest) cannot express an unpublished base — a single `FROM` has no optional
  `@` — so the layer would simply be unbuildable on a machine with no registry.
  Passing the whole reference keeps the dependency visible in one line at both
  ends of the lifecycle: `name:tag` locally, `name@sha256:<manifest list
  digest>` when the base was published, with the digest form required whenever
  the base came from a registry.
- **`USER root` for the install, then `USER ${AGENT_USER}`.** Installing needs
  root; running never does. Using the inherited `AGENT_USER` rather than the
  literal name means a deployment that renames the account edits its base
  instead of every layer.
- **The install step fails on purpose when unfilled.** A layer image with no
  harness would build, publish, and then refuse to start an agent — a failure
  that belongs in the build, not in production.
- **An optional `HEALTHCHECK`.** The base declares none (nothing to probe
  without a harness); the layer is the first place a meaningful check exists.

## Validation

```
docker build -f Containerfile.layer --build-arg AGENT_DEV_BASE_REF=<ref> \
  -t <tag> .                                                    # exits 0
docker image inspect <tag> \
  --format '{{.Config.User}} {{.Config.WorkingDir}} {{json .Config.Entrypoint}}'
                                                # the base's account, workspace, and entrypoint
docker run --rm <tag>                           # the harness starts, as PID 1, in the workspace
```

Both inspections also pass for a layer that restates nothing about the
contract — no `USER`, no `WORKDIR`, no `ENTRYPOINT` — because a `COPY` into a
root-owned directory is performed by the builder, so a layer needs a
`USER root` section only when it installs with a package manager. What no
layer may do is restate a *different* value: `USER ${AGENT_USER}` at the end
of a root section is the switch back to the inherited account, not a second
declaration of it.
